    lesson:
      "The **Columbian Exchange** is the bi-directional transfer of organisms, goods, and people across the Atlantic after 1492.\n\n- **To the Americas**: wheat, rice, sugar, coffee, bananas; horses, pigs, cattle, sheep, chickens; smallpox, measles, influenza, malaria, yellow fever.\n- **To Europe and Africa**: maize, potatoes, tomatoes, cassava, beans, squash, tobacco, cacao; syphilis (probably); enormous quantities of silver and gold.\n\n**Demographic catastrophe in the Americas**: Native populations had no acquired immunity to Eurasian diseases. 50-90% mortality over the first century is the scholarly consensus — the largest demographic collapse in recorded history.\n\n**In Europe**: American foods transformed diets. The **potato** alone may have boosted European population by 25% by 1800 by yielding more calories per acre than any grain. American maize fed African slave-trade ports.\n\n**Price Revolution**: between 1500 and 1650 European prices roughly quadrupled — fueled by an avalanche of silver from **Potosí** (Bolivia, opened 1545) and Mexican mines, combined with population recovery. Wages lagged prices, hurting wage laborers and helping landlords with fixed rents to renegotiate.\n\n**Commercial Revolution** describes the financial and organizational innovations that came with global trade:\n- **Joint-stock companies** (Dutch VOC 1602, English EIC 1600) pooled investor capital and limited liability — the ancestors of modern corporations.\n- **Banks** (Amsterdam Exchange Bank, 1609) handled bills of exchange and deposits, supporting long-distance trade.\n- **Bourses / stock exchanges** (Antwerp, then Amsterdam) traded shares and commodities.\n- **Insurance** (Lloyd's of London, 17th c.) spread shipping risk.\n- **Atlantic slave trade**: triangular trade — European manufactures to Africa, enslaved Africans to the Americas, raw goods (sugar, tobacco, silver) back to Europe — became the engine of the Atlantic economy.\n\n**Mercantilism** is the economic doctrine that justified all this: a state's wealth = its stock of bullion; therefore export more than you import (favorable balance of trade), accumulate gold and silver, and use **colonies** as captive sources of raw materials and markets for finished goods. Tools: protective tariffs, **Navigation Acts** (English, 1651), state-chartered monopolies. Adam Smith would attack mercantilism in 1776 (4.3).",
