    lesson:
      "Unit 3 covers the long century after Westphalia (1648) when European states experimented with two answers to the question religious war had posed: how should sovereign power be organized?\n\n**Absolutism** (Louis XIV, Habsburgs, Hohenzollerns, Romanovs): the monarch is the sole source of law, accountable only to God; estates and parliaments are sidelined; a centralized bureaucracy and standing army enforce uniformity.\n\n**Constitutionalism** (England after 1688, Dutch Republic): sovereign authority is shared among monarch, parliament/estates, and (in some accounts) law itself; rulers are limited by representative bodies and explicit charters or customs.\n\nThese were not modern democracies — most people had no political voice — but they were divergent answers to the post-Westphalia question.\n\n**Material context**:\n- The **17th-century General Crisis**: cooler weather (Little Ice Age), bad harvests, plague recurrences, civil wars (Frondes in France, English Civil War, Catalan and Portuguese revolts). Population stalled or fell across much of Europe c. 1600-1700.\n- Recovery and growth in the **late 17th and 18th centuries**: agricultural innovation, expanding Atlantic trade, urbanization, the rise of consumer culture.\n\n**Geopolitical context**:\n- **France** under Louis XIV (r. 1643-1715) became the dominant European power and the model absolutist state.\n- **Britain** rose as a constitutional monarchy and naval/commercial superpower after 1688.\n- The **Dutch Republic** dominated the 17th century commercially before being overtaken by Britain in the 18th.\n- **Eastern Europe** saw new players: **Brandenburg-Prussia** built a militarized absolutism; **Russia** under **Peter the Great** (r. 1682-1725) westernized aggressively; the **Habsburg** monarchy consolidated against Ottoman retreat; **Poland-Lithuania** declined and was eventually partitioned (1772, 1793, 1795).\n- **Ottoman Empire**'s second siege of Vienna (1683) was repelled — the start of long Ottoman retreat.\n\nThe unit ends in 1815 with the Congress of Vienna (5.7), but the state-building toolkit it tracks — bureaucracy, taxation, standing army, constitutional or absolute justification — defined European politics for the next two centuries.",
