    lesson:
      "England's 17th century is the central case study for how a European state took the **constitutional** path.\n\n**Stuart background**: **James I** (r. 1603-25) preached **divine right** but governed pragmatically. **Charles I** (r. 1625-49) tried to rule without Parliament (the **Personal Rule**, 1629-40), funding government via revived feudal taxes (e.g., **ship money**) and enforcing high-Anglican worship under Archbishop **Laud**. Scottish Presbyterians revolted (1638) when Charles imposed an Anglican prayer book. He recalled Parliament to fund war — beginning the breakdown.\n\n**English Civil War (1642-49)**:\n- **Royalists ('Cavaliers')**: north and west, Anglican and Catholic gentry.\n- **Parliamentarians ('Roundheads')**: south and east, Puritans, urban merchants.\n- **Oliver Cromwell** built the **New Model Army** — disciplined, ideologically Puritan, promoted on merit. Defeated royalists at Naseby (1645).\n- Charles I was captured, tried by a Rump Parliament, and **executed January 1649** — the first European monarch killed by his own subjects in a public legal trial.\n\n**Commonwealth and Protectorate (1649-60)**: England a republic in name; **Cromwell** ruled as **Lord Protector** (1653-58). Crushed Irish Catholic resistance brutally (Drogheda 1649). Suppressed radical groups (Levellers demanding manhood suffrage; Diggers demanding common ownership). After Cromwell's death (1658), military rule unraveled and Parliament invited **Charles II** back (**Restoration, 1660**).\n\n**Restoration tensions**: Charles II (r. 1660-85) navigated Anglican-Dissenter conflict; secretly drew French subsidies. His brother **James II** (r. 1685-88) was openly Catholic, suspended laws against Catholics, and fathered a Catholic son (June 1688) who would inherit. Parliamentary leaders invited James's Protestant daughter **Mary** and her Dutch husband **William of Orange** to invade.\n\n**Glorious Revolution (1688-89)**: William landed in November 1688; James fled to France with virtually no fighting — hence 'glorious' (i.e., bloodless in England, though violent in Ireland and Scotland). Parliament declared the throne vacant and offered it jointly to William III and Mary II under conditions:\n- **Bill of Rights (1689)**: no royal suspension of laws or taxation without parliamentary consent; right to petition; free elections; freedom of speech in Parliament; no standing army in peacetime without consent.\n- **Toleration Act (1689)**: dissenting Protestants could worship freely (Catholics excluded).\n- **Act of Settlement (1701)**: Catholics barred from the throne.\n\n**John Locke's *Two Treatises of Government*** (published 1689) provided the **theoretical justification**: government rests on consent of the governed; rulers who violate natural rights (life, liberty, property) may be replaced. Locke became the canonical theorist of constitutionalism — and a direct influence on the American Revolution (4.3, 4.6).\n\nResult: by 1700 Britain was a **parliamentary monarchy** with secure property rights, a professionalized civil service, and after 1694 the **Bank of England** — institutional foundations of its 18th-century commercial and military rise.",
