    title: "Continuities and Changes to Economic Practice and Development",
    summary:
      "Between 1648 and 1815 Europe's economy shifted from a subsistence agricultural base toward commercialized agriculture, proto-industry, and an Atlantic-centered trading system increasingly dominated by Britain and the Dutch.",
    lesson:
      "Three big economic shifts define the period:\n\n**1. Agricultural Revolution** (Britain especially, c. 1650-1800): higher yields per acre supported population growth and freed labor for industry.\n- **Enclosure**: common fields converted into privately owned, hedged plots — efficient but destroyed customary peasant rights. In Britain accelerated by Parliamentary Enclosure Acts (peak 1750-1830).\n- **Crop rotation**: Charles 'Turnip' Townshend popularized the four-field rotation (wheat, turnips, barley, clover), eliminating the medieval fallow.\n- **Selective breeding**: Robert Bakewell improved sheep and cattle.\n- **New crops**: **potato** (from the Americas) and **maize** doubled or tripled caloric yield per acre, especially in Ireland, the Low Countries, and German lands.\n- **Jethro Tull's seed drill** (1701) and improved plows.\n\nResult: European population grew from ~120 million (1700) to ~190 million (1800) without famine on Malthusian scale — except the Irish Potato Famine of 1845-49 (6.5).\n\n**2. Cottage industry / proto-industrialization**: rural families (especially in Britain's Midlands, the Low Countries, and Saxony) wove cloth, knitted stockings, and made small metal goods on a **putting-out system** — merchants supplied raw materials and bought finished goods. This generated rural cash income, accumulated entrepreneurial capital, and produced the workforce later absorbed by factories (6.2).\n\n**3. Atlantic trade boom**:\n- The **triangular trade** linked European manufactures, African slaves, and American plantation goods (sugar, tobacco, cotton, coffee, indigo). Bristol, Liverpool, Nantes, Bordeaux, and Amsterdam grew rich.\n- The **Atlantic slave trade** moved an estimated 12 million Africans to the Americas, c. 1500-1850 — peak in the 18th century. The trade's profits financed infrastructure, banking, and (some scholars argue) early industrial investment in port cities.\n- **Sugar** was the dominant commodity. Caribbean sugar plantations consumed African lives at horrifying rates and made European fortunes.\n- **Mercantilist regulation** (Navigation Acts, monopolistic chartered companies) channeled trade through metropolitan ports until the late 18th century.\n\n**Continuities**: most Europeans remained peasant agriculturalists; subsistence farming continued in southern, eastern, and Alpine Europe; serfdom remained the norm east of the Elbe.\n\n**Changes**: the share of the workforce in non-agricultural occupations rose; urbanization accelerated (London ~575,000 in 1700 to ~1 million by 1800; Paris similarly); a consumer culture emerged around colonial goods (sugar in tea, coffee, tobacco).",
