    lesson:
      "After winning effective independence from Spain (1581/1609; formal at Westphalia 1648), the **Dutch Republic** of seven provinces (Holland the wealthiest) experienced a 17th-century **Golden Age** disproportionate to its size (~2 million people).\n\n**Political form**:\n- A **federal republic** of seven provinces governed by a **States General**; the most powerful provincial official was the **Stadtholder** (often a member of the **House of Orange**).\n- Rule by a **wealthy urban patriciate** of merchants ('regents'), not the nobility — closer to oligarchy than democracy, but a striking departure from absolutist neighbors.\n\n**Economic dominance**:\n- The **Dutch East India Company (VOC, 1602)** was the world's first multinational joint-stock company and de facto sovereign in much of maritime Asia (Batavia/Jakarta as headquarters).\n- The **West India Company (1621)** competed in Atlantic trade; New Amsterdam (later New York) was a Dutch colony until 1664.\n- **Amsterdam** became Europe's financial capital: the **Bourse** (stock exchange) traded company shares from 1602; the **Bank of Amsterdam (1609)** standardized currencies and offered safe deposits.\n- **Shipbuilding** efficiency (the *fluyt* freighter) and low interest rates gave the Dutch a freight-cost advantage that persisted for decades.\n- **Tulip mania (1636-37)**: history's first well-documented speculative bubble — single tulip bulbs briefly traded for the price of an Amsterdam house.\n\n**Religion and tolerance**:\n- Officially **Calvinist** Reformed Church but with relatively tolerant practice — Catholics, Lutherans, Mennonites, and Sephardic Jews (refugees from Iberian persecution) lived openly. **Spinoza** (Jewish philosopher) and **Descartes** (French Catholic) wrote in Dutch exile.\n\n**Art**: a market-driven, bourgeois Baroque rather than Catholic-Baroque. **Rembrandt van Rijn** (*The Night Watch*, *Self-Portraits*) — group portraits, biblical scenes, intimate psychology. **Johannes Vermeer** (*Girl with a Pearl Earring*, *The Milkmaid*) — domestic interiors with luminous light. **Frans Hals** — portraits. **Jan Steen** — comic genre scenes. **Vanitas** still lifes warned wealthy viewers of mortality and the emptiness of riches.\n\n**Decline**:\n- Costly **Anglo-Dutch Wars** (1652-54, 1665-67, 1672-74) chipped away at trade dominance.\n- Louis XIV's invasion (1672) — the *Rampjaar* ('disaster year') — was repelled only by opening the dikes.\n- After William III became king of England (1689), Dutch resources were diverted to fighting France.\n- By the mid-18th century, Britain had overtaken the Dutch in shipping and finance; Amsterdam remained important but no longer dominant.\n\n**APEH point**: the Dutch are the great 17th-century *constitutionalist* counterweight to Louis XIV's France — a major **comparison** opportunity.",
