    lesson:
      "Britain's 'ascendancy' rested on a self-reinforcing combination of political, fiscal, military, and economic advantages.\n\n**Political-fiscal foundation**:\n- The Glorious Revolution (1688) made Parliament the credible guarantor of property and debt. Investors trusted Britain's promises to repay.\n- The **Bank of England (1694)** managed the national debt; long-term **consols** allowed Britain to borrow at lower rates than absolutist France.\n- The **Treasury** developed a professional bureaucracy efficient at collecting customs and excise.\n- Result: Britain extracted more revenue per capita than France through the 18th century despite a smaller population.\n\n**Military advantage**:\n- The **Royal Navy** dominated maritime warfare from the 1690s. It convoyed merchant fleets, blockaded enemies, and projected power globally.\n- Britain's army was small but supplemented by **subsidies** to continental allies (Prussia in the Seven Years' War; many in the Napoleonic era).\n- **Press gang** filled naval crews; merchant marine doubled as naval reserve.\n\n**Imperial expansion**:\n- **Seven Years' War (1756-63)**: Britain won Canada (Plains of Abraham, 1759), Bengal (Plassey, 1757), and Caribbean islands. Treaty of Paris (1763) made Britain the dominant overseas empire.\n- **Loss of American colonies (1783)** was a setback but Britain quickly rebuilt trade with the new United States. India and the Caribbean remained crucial.\n\n**Commercial-industrial dominance**:\n- **Agricultural Revolution** (3.3) freed labor for industry.\n- **Cottage industry / putting-out** (3.3) created a workforce trained in manufacturing.\n- **Coal and iron** in abundance; steam engine (Watt 1769) increasingly applied to mining and textiles.\n- **Cotton textile industry** in Manchester/Lancashire scaled rapidly after 1780; Britain's spinning and weaving machinery undercut Indian cotton globally.\n- **Atlantic markets** (Africa, Americas, Caribbean) absorbed British exports and supplied raw cotton.\n\n**Constitutional flexibility**: Parliament accommodated both rural landed interests (Tory) and urban commercial interests (Whig). Reform pressures channeled into legislation rather than revolution. (The **Gordon Riots** of 1780 and Luddite protests later showed real strain, but the system held.)\n\n**Religious-cultural assets**:\n- **Toleration Act (1689)** for Protestant Dissenters allowed Quakers, Methodists, and other commercially active groups to operate legally.\n- **Methodist revival** (John Wesley, mid-18th c.) energized working-class religion and indirectly supported reform causes (abolitionism).\n\n**By the early 19th century** (Napoleonic era and after), Britain was the world's leading naval power, leading manufacturer, leading financier, and was about to enter its mid-century role as the global hegemon — a position rooted in choices made between 1688 and 1815.",
