    lesson:
      "Unit 8 covers the **30-year European catastrophe** of 1914-1945 — two world wars and the interwar collapse that connects them. By 1945, ~50-80 million people had been killed in the second war alone; European civilians had been deliberately exterminated in the Holocaust; cities across Europe lay in ruins; the U.S. and USSR had replaced European powers as global hegemons.\n\n**The 30-year arc**:\n- **WWI (1914-18)**: industrial-scale warfare destroyed four empires (Habsburg, Romanov, Ottoman, German) and killed ~10-15 million.\n- **Interwar period (1918-39)**: failed Versailles peace; brief 1920s recovery; **Great Depression (1929)**; rise of **fascism** (Mussolini 1922, Hitler 1933) and **Stalinist** USSR; appeasement.\n- **WWII (1939-45)**: ~50-80 million killed; Holocaust murdered ~6 million Jews and millions of Roma, Soviet POWs, disabled, political prisoners; nuclear weapons used (Hiroshima, Nagasaki, August 1945).\n- **1945 settlement**: Yalta and Potsdam divided Europe into Soviet and Western spheres — the Cold War (Unit 9).\n\n**Why catastrophe?**\n- *Industrial weaponry*: machine guns, artillery, gas, tanks, aircraft, submarines, eventually nuclear weapons made war exponentially deadlier.\n- *Mass conscription and total war*: entire societies mobilized; civilians targeted.\n- *Radical ideologies*: fascism and Stalinism replaced 19th-century liberal-conservative politics with mass parties willing to use unlimited violence.\n- *Failed peace*: the Versailles Treaty (1919) humiliated Germany without disabling it; the League of Nations lacked enforcement; the U.S. retreated into isolation.\n- *Economic instability*: 1920s reconstruction was fragile; the Depression (1929-39) destroyed the political center across Europe.\n- *Imperial overstretch*: European empires drew colonial resources into European war but also exposed European weakness, sowing decolonization.\n\n**Big questions**:\n- Was WWII a direct continuation of WWI? (Many historians treat 1914-1945 as a single 'Thirty Years' War.')\n- Were fascism and Stalinism uniquely 20th-century? Or did they have 19th-century roots?\n- Could the catastrophes have been prevented? (Counterfactuals at every step.)\n\nUnit 8's task is to trace the causal chain — and to show how the apparently progressive Europe of 1900 produced Auschwitz and Hiroshima within 45 years.",
